If you want to see the error messages, try using the serial console.
I think the command was ">>> set console serial" or of course
you can just physically yank the graphics card (power off of course!)
and it will display output to the serial port.  Then hook up some sort
of real terminal or terminal emulator and you will capture the output.

This will give you more info about what the failure is.

If I had to guess, I'd say there's some sort of corruption of 
the file systems on the disk.  Just a guess, of course, and it's
worth what you paid for it.  In that case, it might be worth
finding some other boot media (a different disk perhaps, or
the install cd-roms, etc) and if you boot on the alternate
media you can rule out the file system.  On the other hand,
if the alternate media does boot ok, perhaps this media has
some repair/analysis tools you can use to diagnose/repair 
the issue.

Be very careful that the alternate media and the installed
media are the same version.  Nasty things can happen if you
try to repair a version X file system using tools from version
Y. You do have a good backup, right?

--lw--


