We finally got the thing working. It looked to be something possibly related
either to the BIOS pr the EISA bus.

We were able to take the main boot drive from the machine and successfully
boot a sister AlphaServer 4000. Similarly, if we took that sister's boot
drive to the problem machine, it failed. That seemed to confirm some sort of
hardware/firmware issue.

The HP Tech talked to some of his advanced support resources, who suggested
running some sort of really old and obsolete utility that they used to use
to configure EISA busses - something we don't explicitly have set up or
installed, but it shows up in the boot messages. Anyway, all we did was
enter this utility then just exited. I guess that must have set some
low-level bits somewhere deep in the hardware.

We managed to boot the system after that and didn't have a single bit of
data. All the HP tech's are still scratching their heads over it =P

Thanks for the suggestion!

Cheers,

James.


